Fix $0<t<1/4$ and set $a=2t$. Complementary labels will need to give exactly the same metric, even when the two subsystem dimensions differ. We therefore use the full dimension $d$ in the following formula for every subsystem. Besides having eigenvalues of size $e^{-tF_Q}$ up to polynomial factors, the metric must admit a positive mixture of tensor powers, so that the conditional movement estimate can be applied inside its integral. The ratio between its $k$-copy and $(k-1)$-copy weights must also approximate marginal powers for the local-operator calculation. The following common label function has all these properties. Pad each label to length $d$ and put $$\label{replicas:w-definition}
 \begin{split}
 c&=d+(1+t)\binom{d}{2},\\
 w_k(\lambda)&=\frac{\Gamma(c)}{\Gamma(c+tk)}
    \prod_{i=1}^d
       \frac{\Gamma(1+t(\lambda_i+d-i))}
            {\Gamma(1+t(d-i))},\\
 W_{Q,k}&=\sum_\lambda w_k(\lambda)\pi_{Q,k}^{\lambda}.
 \end{split}$$ In particular $W_{Q,0}=I$. For a one-dimensional subsystem at positive $k$, $W_{Q,k}$ is the positive scalar $w_k((k))$; it need not equal one. This convention keeps the label function identical on all subsystems.

Fix $C_0>0$ and let $\varepsilon,\beta,\alpha$ be as in <a href="#eq:amplification-exponents" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:amplification-exponents">[eq:amplification-exponents]</a>. Suppose $n\ge2$, $L=\lfloor n^{1-\varepsilon}\rfloor$, and $$X=A\cap T,\qquad S_0=A\cap T_L,\qquad Y=S_0\setminus X,
 \qquad Z'=\Lambda\setminus S_0.$$ Assume that $|S_0|\le C_0n^2$, that each of the three cuts $X,Y,S_0$ has at most $C_0n$ crossing edges, and that $$\label{eq:amplification-input-information}
 \mathcal I:=I_\Omega(X:Z')\le C_0n^{1-\varepsilon}.$$ For every integer $M\ge1$, at all sufficiently large $n$, there is an integer radius $r$ such that $$\label{eq:amplification-radius}
 r\le C_M n^{(1-\varepsilon)/\alpha},\qquad
 L+2r=o(n^\beta),$$ and, simultaneously for every physical set $J\subseteq\Lambda\setminus N_{2r}(S_0)$, $$\label{eq:amplification-output}
 I_\Omega(X:J)\le C_M n^{-M}.$$ The same bound holds with any subset of $X$ in place of $X$. The constants and the threshold may depend on $C_0$ and $M$, but are independent of $\Lambda,A,T$ subject to the stated assumptions.

