Let $\theta$ be a unit pure vector on four finite-dimensional systems $P,x,U,F$, and let $\rho_D$ denote its marginal on $D$. Set $$d=\dim x,\quad \ell=\log(ed),\quad
 \eta=S(x|P)_\theta+S(x|U)_\theta
      =I(x:F|U)_\theta=I(x:F|P)_\theta.$$ There are universal constants $c,C>0$ such that, for $0<a\ell\le c$ and arbitrary density matrices $\sigma$ on $xP$ and $\tau$ on $xU$, $$\label{eq:conditional-movement}
 \left\|\sigma^{a/2}\widehat\rho_P^{-a/2}
          \tau^{a/2}\widehat\rho_U^{-a/2}\theta\right\|
 \le\exp\left(-\frac a2\eta+C a^{5/4}\ell^2\right).$$ The same bound holds for positive subnormalized $\sigma,\tau$.

Let $\theta$ be a unit pure vector on finite-dimensional systems $P,x,U,F$, write $\rho_D$ for its marginals, and put $d=\dim x$ and $\eta=I(x:F|U)_\theta$. For $\eta>0$ define $$y=\min(1,\eta),\qquad
 \mathcal R_\eta=\sqrt y+\sqrt{\eta\log(ed/y)},$$ and set $\mathcal R_0=0$. There is a universal constant $C>0$ such that, for every $u\in\mathbb R$, $$\label{eq:conditional-modular-bound}
 \big\|\big(\widehat\rho_U^{-iu}\widehat\rho_{xU}^{iu}
       -\widehat\rho_{UF}^{-iu}\widehat\rho_{xUF}^{iu}\big)
                                            \theta\big\|
 \le C|\sinh(\pi u)|\mathcal R_\eta.$$ In particular the two phase products agree on $\theta$ if $\eta=0$. The constants do not depend on $\dim P$, $\dim U$, or $\dim F$.

For the finite-dimensional tensor systems just defined, the following statements hold.*


1.  *For $\lambda\vdash k$ of length at most $q$, put $l_i=\lambda_i+q-i$ for $1\le i\le q$. Then $$\label{replicas:dimensions}
     \dim V^{(q)}_\lambda
       =\prod_{i<j}\frac{l_i-l_j}{j-i},\qquad
     d_\lambda=\frac{k!\prod_{i<j}(l_i-l_j)}{\prod_i l_i!},\qquad
     \log d_\lambda=kH(\lambda/k)+O_q(\log(k+2)).$$ The number of labels and the sum of their general-linear multiplicities are polynomially bounded in $k$, with $q$ fixed.*

2.  *Central label functions commute for nested or disjoint subsystems. On $\mathcal S_k$, complementary label projectors agree: $\pi_{Q,k}^{\lambda}=\pi_{Q^c,k}^{\lambda}$.*



3.  *If $Q,E$ are disjoint and their labels $\lambda,\mu$ are compatible with label $\nu$ on $QE$, then $$\label{replicas:merge-dimensions}
     d_\nu\le d_\lambda d_\mu,\qquad
     d_\lambda\le d_\nu d_\mu.$$ For a tensor product of permutation-invariant density matrices on $Q^{\otimes k}$ and $E^{\otimes k}$, the conditional probability of $\nu$ given $\lambda,\mu$ is at most $\mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k)d_\nu/(d_\lambda d_\mu)$. Consequently, for $0\le b\le1$, $$\label{replicas:merge-moment}
     \mathbb E\left(\frac{d_\lambda d_\mu}{d_\nu}\right)^b
     \le\mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k),$$ uniformly in those density matrices.*

4.  *Split the copies into specified groups of sizes $k-r$ and $r$. If their labels $\alpha,\beta$ on a fixed subsystem are compatible with the full label $\lambda$, then $$\label{replicas:group-dimensions}
     d_\alpha d_\beta\le d_\lambda
           \le \binom{k}{r}d_\alpha d_\beta.$$ These subgroup label projectors commute with the full label projector.*


5.  *If $\rho$ is any permutation-invariant density matrix on $Q^{\otimes k}$ and $L_\rho=-\log\rho$ on its support, then $F_Q$ commutes with $\rho$, $0\le F_Q\le L_\rho$ on that support, and $$\label{replicas:surprisal-moment}
     \mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\rho\exp\bigl(b(L_\rho-F_Q)\bigr)\le\mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k)
           \qquad(0\le b\le1).$$*


6.  *The full $Q$ label $\lambda$ and the first-$(k-1)$ label $\nu$ are compatible precisely along the possible removable-row branches $\nu=\lambda-e_i$. In any permutation-invariant $Q$ state, conditional on $\lambda$, the probability of this branch is $$\label{replicas:branch-probability}
     p_{\lambda,i}=\frac{d_{\lambda-e_i}}{d_\lambda}
     =\frac{l_i}{k}\frac{\prod_{a<b}(l_a-\delta_{ai}-l_b+\delta_{bi})}
                             {\prod_{a<b}(l_a-l_b)}
     \le 2^q\frac{l_i}{k}.$$ The normalized Jucys–Murphy, or star, operator (Vershik and Okounkov 2005, sec. 2) [[VershikOkounkov2005](#ref-VershikOkounkov2005)] $$\label{replicas:star-definition}
     J_{Q,k}=\frac1k\sum_{j<k}U_Q((j\ k))$$ has eigenvalue $(\lambda_i-i)/k$ on that branch.*

*The polynomial bounds may depend on the fixed subsystem dimensions, but not on the density matrices.

The operators in <a href="#replicas:w-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="replicas:w-definition">[replicas:w-definition]</a> are positive definite and central. Uniformly in their labels, $$\label{replicas:W-comparison}
 \mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k)^{-1}e^{-tF_{Q,k}}\le W_{Q,k}
       \le\mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k)e^{-tF_{Q,k}}.$$ On $\mathcal S_k$, $W_{Q,k}=W_{Q^c,k}$ exactly. For each subsystem there is a probability measure on positive definite subdensities $\sigma$ on $Q$, with $\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}\sigma\le1$, such that $$\label{replicas:W-integral}
 W_{Q,k}=\int(\sigma^t)^{\otimes k}\,d\zeta_Q(\sigma)
                  \qquad(k\ge0).$$ The measure is independent of $k$.*



*Extend $W_{Q,k-1}$ by the identity on the last copy and define $R_{Q,k}=W_{Q,k}W_{Q,k-1}^{-1}$. Then $$\label{replicas:R-forward}
 \lVert R_{Q,k}-(J_{Q,k})_+^t\rVert\longrightarrow0$$ on the entire tensor space. There is a constant $C_{\mathcal V,t}$ such that, for every unit $z\in\mathcal S_k$, $\lVert R_{Q,k}^{-1}z\rVert\le C_{\mathcal V,t}$, and $$\label{replicas:R-inverse}
 \limsup_{k\to\infty}\sup_{\substack{z\in\mathcal S_k\\\lVert z\rVert=1}}
 \lVert \bigl(R_{Q,k}^{-1}-\max(J_{Q,k},\delta)^{-t}\bigr)z\rVert
 \le C_{\mathcal V,t}\delta^{(1-2t)/2}
                 \qquad(0<\delta<1).$$

Let $P,x,Y_0,F$ partition the fixed finite system $\mathcal V$, put $Y=xY_0$, and let $$A_k=A_k(P,xY_0,F),\qquad B_k=A_k(Px,Y_0,F),\qquad
 C_{x,k}=A_k^{-1/2}B_kA_k^{-1/2}.$$ Suppose $0<t<1/4$, $a=2t$, and $a\log(e\dim x)\le c$, with $c$ as in Lemma <a href="#lem:movement" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:movement">13</a>. For every unit $\theta\in\mathcal V$, define $$\eta_\theta=S_\theta(x|P)+S_\theta(x|Y_0)
             =I_\theta(x:F|P)=I_\theta(x:F|Y_0)\ge0.$$ There is a polynomial bound uniform in $\theta$ such that, on $\mathcal S_k$, $$\label{replicas:relative-pin}
 C_{x,k}\ge\mathop{\mathrm{poly}}(k)^{-1}
   \exp\left\{ka\left[\eta_\theta
          -C a^{1/4}\log^C(e\dim x)\right]\right\}
             P_{\theta,k}.$$ The same statement holds for a move to $F$ after exchanging the outer parts. A choice making no move has $C_{x,k}=I$ and may be assigned gain zero.

Let $\mathcal T$ be a weighted binary tree with terminal weights $w_j$ and positive definite inputs $A_j$, and let $M$ be its root. The root commutes with invertible congruence, is monotone in each input, and satisfies $$\label{transport:scalar-homogeneity}
 \mathcal T(c_jA_j:j)=\left(\prod_j c_j^{w_j}\right)M
 \qquad(c_j>0).$$ For every unit vector $z$, $$\label{transport:vector-jensen}
 \langle z,Mz\rangle\leq\prod_j\langle z,A_jz\rangle^{w_j}.$$ If $P$ is an orthogonal projection and $A_j\geq c_jP$ with $c_j>0$, then $$\label{transport:projection-transfer}
 M\geq\left(\prod_jc_j^{w_j}\right)P.$$ Finally, suppose $A_j=\prod_{g=1}^K A_{j,g}$ with positive definite factors, and every factor from band $g$ commutes with every factor from band $g'$ whenever $g\ne g'$. Then $M=\prod_{g=1}^K M_g$, where $M_g$ is the root of the same weighted tree with inputs $A_{j,g}$. The factors $M_g$ commute.

At a binary vertex with output $M$ and a child $D$ of positive edge weight $w$, define $$\label{transport:edge-map}
 \Phi_D(Z)=\frac1w M^{-1/2}
   \partial_D M[D^{1/2}ZD^{1/2}]M^{-1/2}.$$ Here $\partial_D$ is the Fréchet derivative with the other child and the weight fixed. This map is unital and completely positive. Composing these maps from a terminal leaf $j$ to the root gives a unital completely positive map $\Phi_j$ such that $$\label{transport:leaf-map}
 M^{-1/2}\partial_{A_j}M[A_j^{1/2}ZA_j^{1/2}]M^{-1/2}
 =w_j\Phi_j(Z).$$ If all tree inputs are block diagonal for a fixed orthogonal decomposition, these maps preserve each matrix block. On the first diagonal block they are the normalized derivative maps for the tree of first diagonal blocks and do not depend on the other diagonal blocks.

For real $z$, $$\label{transport:g-fourier}
 g_s(z)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{iuz}m_s(u)\,du,
 \qquad m_s(u)>0,\qquad \int_{\mathbb R}m_s(u)\,du=2s.$$ For $s=1/4$, the functions $$\label{transport:h-def}
 h_\pm(z)=\frac{e^{\pm sz}-1}{2\sinh(z/2)}
 =\pm e^{\pm sz/2}g_{s/2}(z)$$ have Fourier densities $$\label{transport:q-density}
 q_\pm(u)=\pm m_{s/2}(u\pm is/2),\qquad
 |q_\pm(u)|\leq\frac1{\sqrt2}m_s(u).$$ The quotients in <a href="#transport:h-def" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="transport:h-def">[transport:h-def]</a> are interpreted continuously at zero.

Fix a finite one-copy space, history and conditional choice trees, partitions, cross-band commutation, positive parameter $a$, and positive contractions with designated supports as specified above. Let $\ell$ bound the logarithmic dimensions of every transferred subsystem and every designated support $D_i$ for which $\mathcal J_i\ne\varnothing$, with the stipulated smallness of $a\ell$. For every $k$ and nonzero $\mathrm{pre}\in\mathcal S_k$, set $$\label{transport:filtered-vector}
 s=\frac14,\qquad
 N(p)=\lVert \mathcal M(p)^{-s}\mathrm{pre}\rVert,\qquad
 v(p)=\frac{\mathcal M(p)^{-s}\mathrm{pre}}{N(p)}.$$ For every $0<p<1$, let $\Phi_j$ be the normalized map from terminal leaf $j$ to $\mathcal M(p)$ and define density matrices $$\label{transport:states}
 \sigma_{j,u}=\Phi_j^*\bigl(
 |\mathcal M(p)^{-iu}v(p)\rangle
 \langle\mathcal M(p)^{-iu}v(p)|\bigr),\qquad u\in\mathbb R.$$ The adjoint here is for the trace pairing: $\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(\Phi_j^*(\sigma)Z)=\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(\sigma\Phi_j(Z))$. Write $\sigma_{h,u}$ for the state at $j=(h,\mathrm{old})$, and put $C_h=A_h^{-1/2}B_hA_h^{-1/2}$. Then $$\label{transport:exact-derivative}
 -\partial_p\log N(p)^2
 =\sum_h w_h\int_{\mathbb R}m_s(u)\mathop{\mathrm{Tr}}(\sigma_{h,u}\log C_h)\,du.$$ There is $\beta_k=O_{\mathrm{fixed}}(\log(k+1))$, independent of $p$ and $\mathrm{pre}$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
\label{transport:entropy-gain}
 -\partial_p\log N(p)^2
 \geq{}&ka\sum_{h,g}w_h\int_{\mathbb R}m_s(u)
    \int\sum_{c\in\mathcal C_h}q_{c\mid h}
          \eta_{h,c,g}(\theta)\,d\mu_{\sigma_{h,u}}(\theta)\,du
 \notag\\[-2pt]
 &{}-CkaK a^{1/4}\ell^C-\beta_k.
\end{aligned}$$ If in addition $\bar H\,\mathrm{pre}=E_0\mathrm{pre}$, then $$\label{transport:energy}
 \langle v,\bar Hv\rangle
 \leq 2E_0+C a^2\ell^C
 \sum_i W_i(p)\sum_{j\in\mathcal J_i}\pi_j
 \int_{\mathbb R}m_s(u)\int\eta_{i,j}(\theta)^{1/8}
                 \,d\mu_{\sigma_{j,u}}(\theta)\,du+o_k(1).$$ The remainder in <a href="#transport:energy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="transport:energy">[transport:energy]</a> is uniform in $p\in(0,1)$, in nonzero $\mathrm{pre}$ satisfying the eigenvector assumption, and in all replica states. The constants $C$ and the exponents denoted by $C$ in <a href="#transport:entropy-gain" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="transport:entropy-gain">[transport:entropy-gain]</a> and <a href="#transport:energy" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="transport:energy">[transport:energy]</a> are universal; support dimensions enter those coefficients only through $\ell$. Only the implicit constant in $\beta_k=O_{\mathrm{fixed}}(\log(k+1))$ and the convergence rate of $o_k(1)$ may depend on the entire fixed finite data. Neither depends on $p$ or the replica state. The entropy estimate can be integrated over a closed subinterval of $[0,1]$ using the continuous endpoint values of $N$.

Fix the positive gapped Hamiltonian and physical target $X$ above, a finite collection of status partitions satisfying <a href="#comparator:nesting" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:nesting">[comparator:nesting]</a>, a typical Schmidt set satisfying <a href="#comparator:typical-set" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:typical-set">[comparator:typical-set]</a>, and a mean tree with metrics <a href="#comparator:metrics" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:metrics">[comparator:metrics]</a>. There is a sequence of common auxiliary labels $\lambda_k$ and nonzero vectors $$\mathrm{pre}_k=\pi_R^{\lambda_k}
  (\widetilde\Omega\otimes\mathrm{Bell}_{CR})^{\otimes k}
       \in\mathcal S_k,
 \qquad \|\mathrm{pre}_k\|\le1,
 \label{comparator:prevector}$$ Here $\pi_R^{\lambda_k}$ is the Schur-label projection on the $R$ copies. These vectors are physical mean-energy eigenvectors of eigenvalue $\widetilde E_0$ and have label $\lambda_k$ on both $C$ and $R$. For every tree above, set $$N=\|\mathcal M^{-s}\mathrm{pre}_k\|,
 \qquad v=\mathcal M^{-s}\mathrm{pre}_k/N,
 \qquad
 E_{\mathrm{def}}=
 \frac{\langle v,\overline{\widetilde H}v\rangle-\widetilde E_0}
      {\widetilde g},
 \qquad
 \overline{\widetilde H}=\frac1k\sum_{i=1}^k\widetilde H^{(i)}.$$ *Rough comparisons.* For $0<\tau<1/2$, the upper bound below uses the prefix data <a href="#comparator:prefix-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:prefix-data">[comparator:prefix-data]</a>: $$\begin{aligned}
 -\frac{\log N^2}{k}
 &\le 2\log d_*-\log z+
       2sW\left(\frac{2B_{\mathrm{sh}}}{z}+2B_{\mathrm{exc}}\right)
       +o_k(1),
 \label{comparator:rough-upper}\\
 -\frac{\log N^2}{k}
 &\ge 2sW\left[
       2S'-C\left(\tau\log d_*+\frac{h(\tau)}a\right)
       -C\frac{E_{\mathrm{def}}}{\tau}S'\right]-o_k(1).
 \label{comparator:rough-lower}
\end{aligned}$$ Here $h$ is binary entropy. The lower bound does not require <a href="#comparator:prefix-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:prefix-data">[comparator:prefix-data]</a> or <a href="#comparator:mgf-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:mgf-data">[comparator:mgf-data]</a>.*




**Sharp comparisons.* If <a href="#comparator:mgf-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:mgf-data">[comparator:mgf-data]</a> holds and $a\le c/\sqrt{\mathcal B}$, with $c$ sufficiently small in terms of $c_0$, define $$\begin{aligned}
 g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{pre}}
   &=S(XU_{j,g})+S(V_{j,g})-S(Y_{j,g}),\\
 g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{post}}
   &=S(U_{j,g})+S(U_{j,g}Y_{j,g})-S(Y_{j,g}),
 \qquad g_{\max}=\max_{j,g}g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{pre}}.
\end{aligned}$$ Then $g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{pre}}\ge0$, and the sharp comparisons are $$\begin{aligned}
 -\frac{\log N^2}{k}
 &\le 2\log d_*-\log z+
  2sW\left[\langle g^{\mathrm{post}}\rangle_{\mathrm{av}}
          +C\left(a\mathcal B+\frac{|\log z|}{a}\right)\right]
          +o_k(1),
 \label{comparator:sharp-upper}\\
 -\frac{\log N^2}{k}
 &\ge 2sW\left[2S'+(1-\tau)
                  \langle g^{\mathrm{pre}}\rangle_{\mathrm{av}}
       -C\left(\tau\log d_*+\frac{h(\tau)}a+a\mathcal B\right)
       -C\frac{E_{\mathrm{def}}}{\tau}(S'+g_{\max})\right]
       -o_k(1).
 \label{comparator:sharp-lower}
\end{aligned}$$ The upper sharp bound does not require <a href="#comparator:prefix-data" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="comparator:prefix-data">[comparator:prefix-data]</a>. For every physical partition there is the exact identity $$2S_X+g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{pre}}-g_{j,g}^{\mathrm{post}}
       =I(X:V_{j,g})+I(X:Y_{j,g}V_{j,g}).
 \label{comparator:entropy-identity}$$*

*All replica limits are taken with the physical system, auxiliary dimensions, finite status collection and tree, $a$, and $\tau$ fixed. The errors are uniform over terminal probabilities, including their dependence on $k$, and over the symmetric vectors to which the lower comparison is applied. Constants or degrees in intermediate polynomial factors in $k$ may depend on the fixed entire system. They disappear in $o_k(1)$. The displayed constants in the rough bounds are universal; those in the sharp bounds may also depend on $C_0,c_0$. None has additional dependence on the system dimensions.

Let positive contractions $k_i$, anchored at $a_i\in\Lambda$ with bounded multiplicity, satisfy $$\|k_i-E_{i,l}k_i\|\le C e^{-c l^\alpha}\qquad(l\ge0),$$ where $E_{i,l}$ is conditional expectation onto $N_l(a_i)$. Set $G_i=(I-k_i)^{1/2}$ and $K_i=k_i^{1/2}$. Fix $t\ge0$ and an integer $r\ge1$. At each anchor label run an independent rate-one Poisson clock on $[0,t]$. In occurrence order, with later factors on the left, let $D$ be the product of all $G_i$, and let $D_{\rm ret}$ retain only events with $d_\Lambda(a_i,S)\le r$, for a nonempty set $S\subseteq\Lambda$. Let $L$ be the same retained product with each $G_i$ replaced by $G_{i,r}=(I-E_{i,r}k_i)^{1/2}$.*



*There are uniform constants $C,c,v>0$ such that $$\label{eq:amplification-product-truncation}
 \mathbb E\|D_{\rm ret}-L\|
 \le Ct|S|(1+r)^2e^{-c r^\alpha}.$$ In particular $L$ is a contraction supported on $N_{2r}(S)$. If $k_i\Omega=0$ and an operator $B$ is supported on $S$ and spectator systems, then, for every unit spectator vector $\xi$, $$\label{eq:amplification-product-omission}
 \mathbb E\|(D-D_{\rm ret})B(\Omega\otimes\xi)\|
 \le Ct|S|\|B\|e^{vt-c r^\alpha}.$$ Both bounds concern the same coupled clock realization. Their constants are independent of $|\Lambda|$ and of spectator dimensions.

Fix $C_0>0$ and let $\varepsilon,\beta,\alpha$ be as in <a href="#eq:amplification-exponents" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:amplification-exponents">[eq:amplification-exponents]</a>. Suppose $n\ge2$, $L=\lfloor n^{1-\varepsilon}\rfloor$, and $$X=A\cap T,\qquad S_0=A\cap T_L,\qquad Y=S_0\setminus X,
 \qquad Z'=\Lambda\setminus S_0.$$ Assume that $|S_0|\le C_0n^2$, that each of the three cuts $X,Y,S_0$ has at most $C_0n$ crossing edges, and that $$\label{eq:amplification-input-information}
 \mathcal I:=I_\Omega(X:Z')\le C_0n^{1-\varepsilon}.$$ For every integer $M\ge1$, at all sufficiently large $n$, there is an integer radius $r$ such that $$\label{eq:amplification-radius}
 r\le C_M n^{(1-\varepsilon)/\alpha},\qquad
 L+2r=o(n^\beta),$$ and, simultaneously for every physical set $J\subseteq\Lambda\setminus N_{2r}(S_0)$, $$\label{eq:amplification-output}
 I_\Omega(X:J)\le C_M n^{-M}.$$ The same bound holds with any subset of $X$ in place of $X$. The constants and the threshold may depend on $C_0$ and $M$, but are independent of $\Lambda,A,T$ subject to the stated assumptions.*


*If in addition $$\label{eq:amplification-cut-clearance}
 \mathop{\mathrm{dist}}_\infty(T,Z)>L+n^\beta,$$ where $Z$ is the set of endpoints of the original cut, then $N_{2r}(S_0)\subseteq A$. In particular the remote set $J$ may include all of $\Lambda\setminus A$.
